p.src:
That moment may be represented as $\text{hundreds of pages written} \Rightarrow \text{severe doubt}.$ The force of the lecture lies in what happens next. The answer is not revelation, self-expression, or panic. It is operational: $\begin{aligned}
\text{doubt} &\Rightarrow \text{press on} \Rightarrow \text{rewrite later if needed},\\
\text{if draft is not good enough} &\Rightarrow \text{rewrite} \Rightarrow \text{improvement}.\end{aligned}$ This is perhaps the lecture’s most practical theorem. A psychological collapse is resolved by procedural trust. One does not stop and let the feeling rule. One continues, protected by the knowledge that revision exists.

